    "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR",
    "body": "### Contributing guidelines\n\n- [X] I have read [CONTRIBUTING.md](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/main/CONTRIBUTING.md)\n- [X] I have read [CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/main/CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md)\n\n### Module(s)\n\nmini.plug\n\n### Description\n\nMost Neovim setups that would include plugins manage them via a plugin/package manager, mainly to ease setup, installation, and synchronization. Popular Lua plugin managers such as [lazy.nvim](https://github.com/folke/lazy.nvim) and [packer.nvim](https://github.com/wbthomason/packer.nvim) exist, but they seem to be too complex. Plugin managers like [paq-nvim](https://github.com/savq/paq-nvim) however, are much simpler (quite a bit of features for only around 500 lines of code). I think it would be beneficial if mini.nvim were to implement some form of a simple plugin/package manager that can be used to keep itself updated, along with maybe some other plugins.\r\n\r\nIn addition, a lazy-loading feature could be skipped, similar to what paq-nvim does, as plugins can be made optional and be `:packadd`-ed later on through the use of an autocommand or keybind. \r\n\r\nThanks",
